 "ses-g7-t1-3": {
  "chapter": "Chapter 1: Introduction to S.E.S.",
  "sections": [
   {
    "h": "",
    "blocks": [
     {
      "t": "p",
      "x": "History & Sources of History"
     },
     {
      "t": "p",
      "x": "History is the study and records of important past events. The three main sources of History are:"
     },
     {
      "t": "ul",
      "items": [
       "Oral Tradition: This is the oldest form of recording history. It involves telling stories by verbal words of the mouth (oral). This source of history is not always accurate/reliable as the story continues from generation to generation; Griots can easily change it through exaggeration or miss out some parts. Such history is usually done by Griots (praise singers).",
       "Written Record: This is the best form of recording history because the facts cannot be easily changed once they are recorded in a form of newspapers, letters, magazines, and photographs. Such written documents are kept at the national ARCHIVE for people to access as reference. An Archive is a place where written documents are kept for reference.",
       "Ancient Remains (Archaeology): Also called digging up history. This involves digging of the ground at different places where people lived many years ago. Articles (artefacts) such as potteries, stones, and metal tools are excavated to study the life of those people. The study of ancient remains is called ARCHAEOLOGY and the person who studies archaeology is called an ARCHAEOLOGIST."
      ]
     }
    ],
    "src": "Chapter 1: Introduction to S.E.S."
   }
  ],
  "text": "history & sources of history history is the study and records of important past events. the three main sources of history are: oral tradition: this is the oldest form of recording history. it involves telling stories by verbal words of the mouth (oral). this source of history is not always accurate/reliable as the story continues from generation to generation; griots can easily change it through exaggeration or miss out some parts. such history is usually done by griots (praise singers). written record: this is the best form of recording history because the facts cannot be easily changed once they are recorded in a form of newspapers, letters, magazines, and photographs. such written documents are kept at the national archive for people to access as reference. an archive is a place where written documents are kept for reference. ancient remains (archaeology): also called digging up history. this involves digging of the ground at different places where people lived many years ago. articles (artefacts) such as potteries, stones, and metal tools are excavated to study the life of those people. the study of ancient remains is called archaeology and the person who studies archaeology is called an archaeologist."
 },
