  "text": "political organisation refers to the structure of a particular administrative set up. the mandinkos have a centralized system of administration (government), and they have 3 lines of authority namely; lineage, which is headed by eldelst male of the family called, “korida tiyo”. village which is headed by the eldest male of the village (founders) called chief or seyfoo state which is headed by the mansa, the highest authority in the mandinko government. the mandinkos have a patrilineal system of inheritance, the king's son or brother succeeds the king. there are 43 districts/chiefs in the gambia, excluding the 3 parts of banjul, that is; banjul north, south, and central (because they don't have a chief). that makes it a total of 43 districts/chiefdoms in the gambia, and 120 wards headed by councillors. modern chiefs have a court called district tribunal (court), where matters relating to marriages and land disputes are judged based on the customary laws. in traditional gambian society, a village head was called chief the head of all chiefs in the gambia is called the paramount chief. the gambia has 53 constituencies and 58 members of parliament or nams, the additional 5 to the 53 are selected / nominated by the president of the day/ republic. the speaker of the house is the political head the symbol of authority of the mandinko chiefs is a stick as their insignia. the gambia is divided into 5 administrative local government regions and 2 municipalities. namely; w.c.r- (brikama), n.b.r- (kerewan), l.r.r -.(mansakonko) , u.r.r. (basse) , c.r.r. (janjanbureh). they are all headed by governors but banjul and kanifing are all headed by a mayor or mayoress. insignia an insignia describes a symbol or badge that makes someone a holder of an important position or authority in the society. in pre-colonial days, kings, chiefs, and alkalos had special sticks (or staff sticks) as their symbol of authority. among the mandinko chiefs, the stick was their symbol of authority, other symbols include horns, flutes, beads etc in the gambia today, modern insignias are awarded in a form of merits and certificates. some renowned / colonial chiefs in the gambia before independence matarr ceesay - upper saloum-njan, (pre-colonial chief), 1935 cherno kaddy baldeh banna-fulladu- sankulay kunda, (pre-colonial chief) pa ture sanyang - faraba bantang – kombo east, (pre-colonial chief), 1939 mama tamba jammeh - baddibu illiyasa, (pre-colonial chief), 1925 abu khan - jokadu-kuntaur, (pre-colonial chief), 1939 sora darboe - jarra bureng, (pre-colonial chief), 1931 pa sanjally bojang - kombo central-kembujeh, village (post colonial chief), 1978 [ diagram / map placeholder ]title: insignia & paramount chief mama tamba jammehdescription: photographs depicting traditional chief insignia and a portrait/historical overview of seyfo mama tamba jammeh. duties or functions of chiefs or “seyfoo” (modern and traditional) duties or functions of chiefs or “seyfoo” (modern and traditional) traditional chiefs modern chiefs in charge of the village, town or hamlet in charge of a district gives land only the alkalo gives land, not the chief collects king's or british governor's taxes collects district tax through the alkalos settle minor disputes at village, town or hamlet levels has a district tribunal or customary court for marriages, inheritance & land disputes represents the village at the king's council oversees development projects in the district make sure customs and traditions are respected make sure customs and traditions are respected preside over village ceremonies preside over district ceremonies functions of alkalos today head of village or hamlet gives land to people collect tax at village level settle village disputes represent the village preside over village ceremonies note: - in today's gambia, some women alkalos can be seen in our communities. such examples are found in; farato, and talokoto, (wcr), juffereh, (nbr), tendaba, (lrr), kabakamma (basse), njawara, (baddibu), mariama kunda, and tungena (kuloro), (wcr), jarra sutukung, (lrr), niumi jasure, (nbr), etc.- constituencies are electoral districts headed by nams, (national assembly members). political organisation refers to the structure of a particular administrative set up. the mandinkos have a centralized system of administration (government), and they have 3 lines of authority namely : lineage, which is headed by eldest male of the family called, “korida tiyo” . village which is headed by the eldest male of the village (founders) called chief or seyfoo . state which is headed by the mansa, the highest authority in the mandinko government . functions of a king / “mansa” to rule and manage the day to day affairs of the kingdom . to defend or protect the kingdom / state . solve dispute . the 43 districts / chiefdoms in the gambia brikama north jarra west janjanbureh brikama south jarra central nianija kombo east jarra east niani kombo south niamina west upper saloum sanementereng niamina dankunku lower saloum busumbala niamina east upper baddibu old yundum lower fulladu west central baddibu foni berefet upper fulladu west lower baddibu foni bintang karanai kantora jokadu foni kansala wuli east upper niumi foni bondali wuli west lower niumi foni jarrol sandu sabach sanjal kiang west sami jimara kiang central basse fulladu east kiang east tumana [ diagram / map / image placeholder ]title: map of regions in the gambiadescription: map showing western, north bank, lower river, central river, and upper river regions . the 53 constituencies in the gambia (electoral districts) examples include: 1. bakau, 2. banjul central, 3. banjul north, 4. banjul south, 5. basse, 6. brikama north, 7. brikama south, etc . nominated nams in the gambia are 5 in number, selected/nominated by the sitting president of the day . the political head of the house/national assembly is called the speaker of the house . wollof political organisation the wollofs have a powerful ruler called “bur” (with a centralized system) of government . the king is helped by a special council of elders/ nobles called “jambur”/ kings maker . the one in charge of all women affairs in the wollof society is called “linguere” meaning a queen or noble woman. she is either the king's mother or sister . functions of jambur / kings maker they appoint/crown the king . they dismiss the king . advice the king . they serve as ministers to the bur's cabinet . functions of the linguere she settles all matters relating to women in court . she organised some villages to pay tribute to the king by working on the royal farm . she adviced the bur/ king . note: the wollofs system of inheritance was matrilineal (mother line) . in wollof society some slaves can hold political powers; such slave classes were called “jami bur jam” / royal slave (they perform the same functions as the jambur/ kings maker, including tax collection, messengers, mediators etc.) . fula political organisation fulas called their leaders or kings as almamy / imam . the imam served as head of a village, religious leader, and protection (lead them in battles) etc . they have patrilineal system of inheritance . the fula states were also called immamates .eg. futa toro, futa djallon, macina, bondu, fulladu, timbo, labe, etc . the fulas established a theocratic system of governments, meaning they rule according to islamic laws [sharia] under a religious leader called imam . the fulas also called their leaders or nobles as saltigi before the coming of islam at futa toro . functions of the fula imam settle disputes . lead them in wars . religious leader who lead them in prayers . settle marriages ."
