  "text": "jola political organisation the jolas did not organised themselves into states or kingdoms. therefore they are fragmented, stateless, disorganised, classless, segmented or decentralized . the jolas lived in separate villages and only comes together in times of crises [war] and “futampaf” and select a leader called “kanda”, who also served as a political and religious leader . they also have a spiritual leader called “ayi” who is always in secret gloves and not easily seen . functions of the jola “ayi” served as a spiritual leader who provides spiritual protection for the village . mediator between the gods and the “jola” people . a liaison officer in times of war, famine or new planting season . functions of the jola “kanda” a political leader . religious leader . he protect the villagers in terms of attacks [war] . settle disputes [quarrels] . serer political organisation the serers had a political structure similar to the wollofs, because they have a common ancestry . they called their chief as “laman” and their kings as “maad”. some mandinko nyanchoes who were rulers among the serers were called “gelewar” as a serere king or noble . some serer states were also ruled by a woman called “linguere” or queen who had the same functions with that of the wollofs linguere . they had a matrilineal system of inheritance . the 2 major serer states were called sine and saloum, originally called mbissil . serahulis political organisations the serahulis also did not established any state in senegambia they only have clan or village heads . a serahuli noble or central figure was called “saltigi” . the political structures of other ethnic groups in the gambia: the akus, manjagos, balantas, bambara, mankaji, and bainunkas are ethnic groups that does not established any state or kingdom in senegambia ."
