  "text": "language: is a system of communication through the use of symbols, sounds and speeches, a collection of sound that are understood by a group of people to have the same meaning . language consists of the words we used to express thoughts and feelings. we put the words together in a special ways so that together the words tell another person what we wish to express . elements of every language vocabulary: this consists or refers to the words and their meanings . grammar: this is the way we combine words so that our meaning is clear. in other words grammar refers to the way words are arranged to make a sense . dialect: this is the variations or differences in a language. dialect is the local form of a language . the jola language has many dialects, eg; djamat, fogni, jola cassa, kaborous, ayat, bayat, bliss, flup, hem, brin, djougout, seleky, elinkin etc . some dialect groups among the fulbe are; fulbe fouta, fulbe torodbe, fulbe firdu, fulbe jerry, etc. some people believe that the fulbe have over 10 dialects in senegambia . some examples of dialects in the gambia jola — \"come\": “joe”, “bil” wolof — \"come\": “dikkal”, “nyowal”, “kai” mandinko — \"bucket\": “sijulo”, “kulujilo” fula — \"shirt\": “wutteh”, “deleke” examples of gambian languages \"good morning\" — olof: “jamu ngam”; mandinka: “i sama”; pularr: “waleh jam”; jola: “g- safu” \"how are you?\" — olof: “na- ngadef”; mandinka: “i be nyadi”; pularr: “nu- way”; jola: “keti sindi” \"thank you\" — olof: “jere- jef”; mandinka: “abaraka”; pularr: “jaaraama”; jola: “ar baraka” \"watch out\" — olof: “moitul”/”outul”; mandinka: “ekeku/ehakilotou”; pularr: “reh- noo”; jola: “kanum” official language this is the language adopted for use by the government in conduct of business and publication of documents . the official language of the gambia is the english language, which has helped to unite all the different ethnic groups of the gambia into one unique nation . importance of the english language in the gambia it unites the country into one nation . it is learn in school systems . it is spoken internationally . it is use to write government documents . note: some written languages in the world are: english language, arabic, french, spanish, etc . lingua franca this is the language that is spoken commonly during trade and commerce, (that is buying and selling of goods and services). the main lingua franca of the gambia is the wolof language . however, lingua franca is subjected to changes depending on the business language of that place. eg; english, fula, mandinka, etc . word borrowing this is the borrowing and use of words from one language to another, as a result of our contact with the europeans and arabs. some west african languages have french, english, portuguese and arabic languages in them. this happened when no other suitable word exist in the original language . some borrowed words into our gambian local languages some borrowed words into our gambian local languages borrowed from english borrowed from english borrowed from french borrowed from french borrowed from portuguese borrowed from portuguese borrowed from arabic banana tabule (table)lunette (speactacle) kojaro (spoon) fajr, subhe, zuhur, magrib blue alumette (match box) kalero (cooking pot) mimbar cigarette marche (market)rendezvous (appointment) guyabo (guyava) “are” salamualaikum sugar griot (praise singers) seyfo (chief) mobile siwo (bucket) radio kompin (club) telephone pompetere (irish potato)findefeer (iron ware) mother tongue this is the language that is mostly spoken at homes by family members. eg; mr. dibba naturally belongs to the mandinka descent, but speaks wolof with all the family members; therefore his mother tongue will be wolof . linguist a linguist is an expert in languages; a person who speaks different languages is called linguist / polygot ."
