  "text": "what is religion? religion generally, is a system of belief and mode of worship directed towards a supreme being, called god, to whom obedience, service and honour is due. eg; islam, christianity, hinduism etc . what is traditional religion? before the coming of arabs (islam) and europeans (christianity) to africa, their religion was called african traditional religion or animism. this is the worshipping of inanimate (lifeless) objects, animals, plants, rivers, stones, mountains, idols and natural events like thunderstorm, earthquake, eclipse etc . they belief there is a supreme being (god) but these objects have spirits or supernatural powers in them, that can serve as an assistance to god. the followers of this religion were called soninkes . types of beliefs monotheism; this is the belief that only one supreme god exists, and the followers are called monotheist. eg; islam polytheism; this is the belief in the existence of more than one god (plurality).the followers are called polytheist trinity; this is the belief in the union of three in one god (the father, the son and the holy spirit), all in one godhead. the followers are called trinitarians. eg; christianity atheism; this is the belief that no god exist (godlessness) and the followers are called atheist some traditional religious practices in the gambia visiting the sacred crocodile ponds in the gambia. eg; bakau kachikally crocodile pond, kmc visiting the stone circles. eg; wassu, kerr bach, etc belief in witches (witchcraft) the use of “jujus” for protection libation and idol worshipping incantation (human and animal sacrifice) visiting tombs and holy men. eg; musa molloh's tomb at kesser kunda, crr worshipping of trees, eg the suspended “kaaba” tree at kiang bateling, lrr some sacred or traditional worshipping places in the gambia the stone circles: they are laterite rocks founded nearly 500 yrs ago, at wassu, kerr bach, dingarai, njie kunda, genji wolof, nianimaru, bantanto ebrima kah(near kaur), wanarr, singai . they are mostly found in the northern part of, (c.r.r.). they existed from 300 bc to 1300 ad; they were the earliest form of human civilisation in the gambia . the stone circles were used as burial places of our kings, chiefs, royal family members and other important members of the community . importance of the stone circles a) serves as a burial ground b) serve as tourist attraction c) attract traditional worshippers d) serves as historical remains (preservation of culture) e) provision of income for the gov't f) evidence of oldest human civilization in the gambia g) source of employment [ diagram / map / image placeholder ]title: wassu stone circle & kenekene mosquedescription: images showing the wassu stone circle monoliths in crr and the kenekene mosque at kombo gunjur . the crocodile ponds, at bakau (kachikally), (own by the bojang clan), niumi (berending), and kartong (folonko) . these crocodile ponds were visited by barren women and other members of the society to pray and wish for a solution to their problems by taking bath with the waters . [ diagram / map / image placeholder ]title: sacred crocodile pondsdescription: images showing bakau kachikally crocodile pond and kartong folonko crocodile pond . “sanneh mentereng” at brufut, it was belief to be a muslim devil that lived at brufut near the sea. jihadist like kombo sillah used to visit the place for prayers before he waged any holy war. the place is still visited by people to pray for their wishes . the big old “mysterious” cooking pot, at foni (bwiam), the cooking pot used to revolve once every year in the olden days. it was believed that wherever its mouth faces may experience conflict, war, famine, disease outbreak etc, in that year . “kenyeh kenyeh” mosque (masjid), at kombo gunjur. the jihadist, sheikh omar futi taal was said to have spent 40 days in prayers at gunjur before his jihad. today, muslims go there on fridays to pray and seek for their wishes . the suspended “kaba” tree at kiang bateling (lrr), this tree is spiritually suspended in the air . “jambar sanneh” in brikama nyambai . “taba konkorong” in (c.r.r) north . “worso” at nuimi (n.b.r) . what is religion? religion generally, is a system of belief and mode of worship directed towards a supreme being, called god, to whom obedience, service and honour is due . african traditional religion / animism: worshipping of inanimate objects, animals, plants, rivers, stones, mountains, idols and natural events like thunderstorm, earthquake, eclipse, etc. followers were called soninkes . types of beliefs monotheism: belief that only one supreme god exists (e.g. islam) . polytheism: belief in the existence of more than one god . trinity: belief in the union of three in one god (father, son, holy spirit; e.g. christianity) . atheism: belief that no god exists (godlessness) . some traditional religious practices & sacred places visiting sacred crocodile ponds (bakau kachikally, niumi berending, kartong folonko) . stone circles: wassu, kerr bach, dingarai, njie kunda, genji wolof, nianimaru, bantanto ebrima kah, wanarr, singai (crr north bank; 300 bc–1300 ad; burial grounds for royalty, oldest evidence of human civilization in the gambia) . sanneh mentereng at brufut, mysterious cooking pot at foni bwiam, kenyeh kenyeh mosque at kombo gunjur (sheikh omar futi taal), suspended kaba tree at kiang bateling, jambar sanneh in brikama nyambai, taba konkorong, worso at nbr . unesco world heritage sites: wassu, kerr batch, kankurang museum, etc . importance of traditional religious practices & sacred places links people to their ancestors . preserves the customs and traditions of the people . source of income/revenue for the custodians and government . brings fortune to the person/people doing it . traditional gods can punish offenders (e.g. “jalang”) . tourist attraction and center for learning/religious practices . roles of a priest / priestesses to perform ritual ceremonies (e.g. initiation) . protect people from evil spirit . settle dispute . serve as advisers . serve as mediators . serve as healers . lead in prayers . traditional medicines this practice involves the use of herbs (roots, leaves, bark of trees, magic) and incantations (human and animal sacrifice) for curing people. the practitioner is called a herbalist, traditional healer, or witch doctor . traditional medicines and the diseases they cure traditional medicines and the diseases they cure traditional medicines “jambakatong” leaf “ratt” for vision and blood “iroko” leaves for chest pain “sinjanoo” “senjen” roots for stomach-ache bitter cola nuts for male sexual disinfection / potency, cold/fever, weight loss, healthy lung, immunity, glaucoma guava leaf for diarrhoea bark of a mango tree for tooth ache “wolo koyo” for stomach ache lemon leaves/ grass for chest pain fresh newly mango leave for tetanus note: a mandinka magician is called “mama jumbo” . importance of traditional medicines it preserves our cultures . can cure diseases caused by witches . can cure some diseases by chance . can be easily accessible . problems / comparison: traditional medicines vs modern medicines problems / comparison: traditional medicines vs modern medicines traditional medicines no exact dose is given not easy to get no long term effect could be risky if abuse no expiry date some have mass products (fake) not scientifically tested tested but expensive to get many side effects less side effects heals some diseases by chance diagnosis is intense"
